  "id": "Compound::DB01567 - Gene::2554",
  "verbalization": "Fludiazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr3, Gabrr2, and Gabrg1, which are paralogs of the Gaba-a receptor subunit Gabra1. These interactions are supported by a consistent score of 0.592 across three distinct paths, indicating a plausible and significant relationship. The observed binding to these related receptors suggests that Fludiazepam may modulate Gaba-a receptor activity, which is critical for synaptic inhibition and neurophysiological regulation. The presence of multiple binding sites on related receptors implies that Fludiazepam could exert its effects through a mechanism involving the Gaba-a receptor complex, potentially influencing neuronal excitability. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Fludiazepam and Gabra1, though the exact functional role of the receptor paralogs remains to be fully elucidated.",
